      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's an interesting way to frame it—as a \"scalable\" approach.\n\nBut I want to dig a bit deeper into the \"varying levels of familiarity\" you mentioned for the Nigeria segment. If we design an interface that is intentionally simplified for them, aren't we essentially creating a tiered experience where the \"high-end\" or \"pro\" version of our product is qualitatively different for people in the Global South compared to the \"standard\" version for someone in London or Tokyo?\n\nIn a fintech context, \"simplicity\" can sometimes be interpreted as \"basic\" or \"limited.\" If we give User C a stripped-back, lower-complexity UI because we assume they need to \"start simple,\" are we not essentially gatekeeping the more powerful, data-driven tools that help people actually build wealth? Is there a risk that by trying to be \"inclusive\" or \"context-aware,\" we are actually reinforcing a digital divide where certain regions are perpetually stuck with \"lite\" versions of technology?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
