      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You bring up a crucial consideration. Establishing a foundational level of simplicity doesn’t have to be the end of a spectrum, where one end represents \"lite\" features for what you might call the \"Global South\" and the other features like \"full\" for the \"Global North.\" Instead, we should consider the possibility of designing a modular and customizable interface, regardless of which market we're engaging with. \n\nImagine a design that starts with core functionality that's universally accessible, explaining essential concepts without the need for prior financial knowledge. From this core, users can intuitively explore additional layers of functionality in a non-disruptive, step-by-step manner. This journey through more complex features would be like accessing more advanced courses after understanding the basics of a language. \n\nTo avoid a tiered experience, you could implement a \"progressive disclosure\" approach where advanced functions don't simply appear; users feel empowered to delve into them at their level of interest and proficiency. Market-specific user journeys would then organically expand towards the full suite of tools as users gain confidence and comfort in their new financial landscape.\n\nIn this way, the simplicity observed in the initial design phase is a gateway, not a limitation. By creating thoughtful pathways that support skill-building and knowledge acquisition, the design",
